        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.352,
            0.026,
            0.853,
            0.043
          ],
          "content": "Chapter 9 — The Amazing World of Solutes, Solvents, and Solutions"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.888,
            0.029,
            0.922,
            0.044
          ],
          "content": "135"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.072,
            0.745,
            0.184
          ],
          "content": "You must have taken an Oral Rehydration Solution (ORS) at some time in your life. ORS is used to treat dehydration by keeping your body hydrated. You have learnt to prepare ORS at home in Curiosity, Grade 6. You may have wondered why every sip of your homemade ORS tastes the same, no matter how much you drink. Why does it not taste salty in one sip and sweet in another?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.787,
            0.079,
            0.888,
            0.194
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.185,
            0.743,
            0.241
          ],
          "content": "This is because when you add sugar and salt to water, they form a mixture in which the components are evenly distributed throughout."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.749,
            0.199,
            0.927,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 9.1: Mixture of sugar, salt, and water"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.242,
            0.674,
            0.299
          ],
          "content": "Can you **predict** whether this mixture is uniform or not (Fig. 9.1)? What happens when chalk powder is mixed with water—does it form a uniform mixture?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.3,
            0.675,
            0.393
          ],
          "content": "When salt and sugar are mixed with water, a **uniform mixture** is formed, whereas when chalk powder or sand, or sawdust is mixed with water, the components are not evenly distributed. Such mixtures are known as **non-uniform mixtures** (Fig. 9.2a and 9.2b)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.394,
            0.661,
            0.415
          ],
          "content": "Let us **explore** the science of mixing things together."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.711,
            0.246,
            0.927,
            0.371
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.686,
                0.375,
                0.943,
                0.407
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 9.2: Mixture of (a) Sand and water; (b) Sawdust and water"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.44,
            0.706,
            0.466
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "9.1 What Are Solute, Solvent, and Solution?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.481,
            0.528,
            0.629
          ],
          "content": "A uniform mixture, such as that of salt or sugar, and water, is called a solution. Whenever a solid is mixed with a liquid to form a solution, the solid component is called the **solute**, and the liquid component is called the **solvent**. The solute dissolves in the solvent to form a **solution** (Fig. 9.3)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.63,
            0.469,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": "Solute + Solvent Solution"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.648,
            0.526,
            0.76
          ],
          "content": "When a solution is formed by mixing two liquids, it is not always clear which substance is dissolving the other. In such cases, the substance present in smaller amount is called the solute, while the one in larger amount is called the solvent."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.539,
            0.475,
            0.936,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.583,
                0.679,
                0.913,
                0.712
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 9.3: Magnified schematic picture of a solute evenly distributed in a solvent"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.797,
            0.432,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": "We know air is a mixture. Would a mixture of gases also be considered a solution?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.439,
            0.764,
            0.59,
            0.895
          ],
